  "derivation": "theorem HasVal.mem {f : F} {c : k} (h : HasVal v f c) : f \u2208 v.toValuationSubring := by\n  have h1 := add_mem (ValuationSubring.nonunits_le (A := v.toValuationSubring) h) (v.algebraMap_mem' c)\n  rwa [sub_add_cancel] at h1",
  "proof_uses_it": true,
  "explanation": "Unfolding the library-local definition `HasVal v f c := f - algebraMap k F c \u2208 v.toValuationSubring.nonunits`, the theorem says: if `f - algebraMap k F c` is a nonunit of the valuation subring `A = v.toValuationSubring`, then `f \u2208 A`. Mathlib's `ValuationSubring.nonunits_le : A.nonunits \u2264 A.toNonUnitalSubring` (equivalently `ValuationSubring.nonunits_subset : (A.nonunits : Set K) \u2286 A`) is precisely the substantive ingredient: it yields `f - algebraMap k F c \u2208 A`; together with the ambient `Place` field `algebraMap_mem' c` (constants lie in `A`) and generic closure glue (`add_mem`, `sub_add_cancel`, i.e. `f = (f - c) + c`), this gives the goal in two short lines. Since `HasVal` and `AlgebraicCurve.Place` are library-local (Mathlib has no `AlgebraicCurve` namespace), no Mathlib declaration states the result verbatim, but the content \u2014 nonunits of a valuation subring lie in it \u2014 is carried by `ValuationSubring.nonunits_le`, which the current proof already applies via dot notation `v.toValuationSubring.nonunits_le`.",
